<section class="tz-hero"><div class="tz-w"><p class="tz-lab">Issue 01 &mdash; Getting dressed, simplified</p><h1>Clothes that fit.<br>Outfits that <mark>work.</mark></h1><div class="tz-hero-g"><p class="tz-intro">Threadz is an independent style guide for people who would like getting dressed to take three minutes. Find your real size, pick an outfit formula for the day and pack a bag that covers the whole trip.</p><p><a class="btn" href="#tools">Open the tools</a></p><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1786118791511-0252f7c4f156?auto=format&fit=crop&w=900&h=600&q=75" alt="row of assorted garments hanging on a metal rail" width="900" height="600" fetchpriority="high"></figure></div></div></section>

<section class="tz-band" aria-label="Rules of thumb"><div class="tz-band-in"><span><b>3</b> measurements decide your size</span><span><b>2&ndash;3</b> outfit formulas are enough</span><span><b>5-4-3-2-1</b> packs a week in a carry-on</span><span><b>1</b> good tailor beats ten new shirts</span></div></section>

<section class="tz-s"><div class="tz-w"><p class="tz-lab">01 / Formulas</p><h2>Three outfit formulas that rarely fail</h2><div class="tz-forms"><article><span>A</span><h3>Fitted + loose</h3><p class="tz-eq">slim top + wide trousers + flat shoe</p><p>Balance is most of style. If one half of the outfit is close to the body, let the other half have volume.</p></article><article><span>B</span><h3>Column + third piece</h3><p class="tz-eq">top and bottom in one colour + contrasting jacket</p><p>A single colour from shoulder to hem lengthens the body. The jacket, cardigan or overshirt adds the interest.</p></article><article><span>C</span><h3>Plain + one statement</h3><p class="tz-eq">neutral basics + one bold item</p><p>A patterned shirt, a bright shoe or a strong coat. Only one, with everything else kept quiet around it.</p></article></div></div></section>

<section class="tz-s tz-blk" id="tools"><div class="tz-w"><p class="tz-lab">02 / Tools</p><h2>Three tools. Open one.</h2><div class="tz-acc">
<details open><summary><span>T1</span>Outfit Formula Picker</summary><div class="tz-tool"><div class="tz-in"><label for="of-o">Where are you going?<select id="of-o"><option value="work">The office</option><option value="weekend">A relaxed weekend</option><option value="dinner">Dinner out</option><option value="travel">A day of travel</option><option value="interview">An interview</option></select></label><label for="of-w">The weather is<select id="of-w"><option value="hot">Hot</option><option value="mild" selected>Mild</option><option value="cold">Cold</option><option value="wet">Wet</option></select></label></div><div class="tz-out" id="of-r" aria-live="polite"></div></div></details>
<details><summary><span>T2</span>Size Finder</summary><div class="tz-tool"><div class="tz-in"><label for="sf-c">Size chart<select id="sf-c"><option value="w">Women&rsquo;s</option><option value="m">Men&rsquo;s</option></select></label><label for="sf-b">Chest or bust (cm)<input id="sf-b" type="number" min="60" max="160" value="92" inputmode="numeric"></label><label for="sf-w">Waist (cm)<input id="sf-w" type="number" min="50" max="160" value="76" inputmode="numeric"></label><label for="sf-h">Hips (cm)<input id="sf-h" type="number" min="60" max="170" value="100" inputmode="numeric"></label></div><div class="tz-out" id="sf-r" aria-live="polite"></div></div></details>
<details><summary><span>T3</span>Trip Packing Planner</summary><div class="tz-tool"><div class="tz-in"><label for="tp-n">Nights away<input id="tp-n" type="number" min="1" max="30" value="6" inputmode="numeric"></label><label for="tp-l">Laundry on the trip?<select id="tp-l"><option value="0">No</option><option value="1">Yes</option></select></label><label for="tp-c">Climate<select id="tp-c"><option value="warm">Warm</option><option value="cool">Cool</option><option value="mixed">Mixed</option></select></label></div><div class="tz-out" id="tp-r" aria-live="polite"></div></div></details>
</div></div></section>

<section class="tz-s"><div class="tz-w"><p class="tz-lab">03 / In practice</p><h2>What a working wardrobe looks like</h2><div class="tz-trip"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1777307224150-2d8535401a53?auto=format&fit=crop&w=520&h=680&q=75" alt="clothes and a straw hat on a clothing rack" width="520" height="680" loading="lazy"><figcaption>A rail you can see all of is a wardrobe you will actually wear.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1777394615575-ec1da2f97c31?auto=format&fit=crop&w=520&h=520&q=75" alt="stack of folded clothes held in a hand" width="520" height="520" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Fold knitwear. Hangers stretch the shoulders.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1708242355178-d8c929b01a9d?auto=format&fit=crop&w=520&h=760&q=75" alt="woman walking down a street" width="520" height="760" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The test of an outfit is whether you forget about it by lunchtime.</figcaption></figure></div></div></section>

<section class="tz-s tz-lime"><div class="tz-w"><p class="tz-lab">04 / Fit</p><h2>Five places to check before you keep it</h2><div class="tblw"><table class="tz-tbl"><thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>Right</th><th>Wrong</th><th>Can a tailor fix it?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Shoulders</td><td>Seam on the shoulder bone</td><td>Seam drooping or pulling</td><td>Rarely</td></tr><tr><td>Chest</td><td>Buttons close flat</td><td>Fabric pulls into an X</td><td>No, size up</td></tr><tr><td>Sleeve</td><td>Ends at the wrist bone</td><td>Covers the knuckles</td><td>Yes, easily</td></tr><tr><td>Waist</td><td>Two fingers of room</td><td>Gapes at the back</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Hem</td><td>Breaks once on the shoe</td><td>Pools on the floor</td><td>Yes, easily</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section>

<section class="tz-s"><div class="tz-w"><p class="tz-lab">05 / Reading</p><h2>The guides</h2><ol class="tz-list"><li><a href="how-to-measure-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1764437835706-ba9d63250bd2?auto=format&fit=crop&w=220&h=150&q=75" alt="textured beige fabric" width="220" height="150" loading="lazy"><span class="tz-no">01</span><span class="tz-ti">The Measure &amp; Fit Guide<small>How to measure yourself, read a size chart and judge fit in the mirror.</small></span><span class="tz-ar">&rarr;</span></a></li><li><a href="travel-packing-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1786413688779-bfbf2674d0b0?auto=format&fit=crop&w=220&h=150&q=75" alt="stacks of folded clothing on a closet shelf" width="220" height="150" loading="lazy"><span class="tz-no">02</span><span class="tz-ti">The Travel Packing Guide<small>The 5-4-3-2-1 method, a colour plan and how to fold so nothing creases.</small></span><span class="tz-ar">&rarr;</span></a></li></ol></div></section>

<section class="tz-s tz-blk"><div class="tz-w"><p class="tz-lab">06 / Packing</p><h2>5-4-3-2-1</h2><div class="tz-count"><div><b>5</b><span>tops</span></div><div><b>4</b><span>bottoms</span></div><div><b>3</b><span>layers &amp; extras</span></div><div><b>2</b><span>pairs of shoes</span></div><div><b>1</b><span>bag</span></div></div><p class="tz-cnote">A week of outfits from fifteen pieces, provided every top goes with every bottom. Choose two neutral colours and one accent before you pack anything.</p></div></section>

<section class="tz-s"><div class="tz-w tz-fq"><div><p class="tz-lab">07 / Questions</p><h2>Asked a lot</h2></div><div class="tz-faq"><details open><summary>How do I find my clothing size?</summary><p>Measure your chest or bust, natural waist and hips with a soft tape, then compare each number with the brand&rsquo;s own chart. Sizes are not standard between labels, so the measurements matter more than the letter on the tag.</p></details><details><summary>What if my top and bottom sizes are different?</summary><p>That is normal, and most people are. Buy tops by your chest or bust and bottoms by the larger of your waist and hip. For dresses and jumpsuits, fit the largest measurement and have the rest taken in.</p></details><details><summary>How should a shirt or jacket fit across the shoulders?</summary><p>The shoulder seam should sit at the edge of your shoulder bone. If it droops down the arm the garment is too big; if it sits inside the bone it is too small. Shoulders are the hardest part to alter, so get them right first.</p></details><details><summary>How many outfits should I pack for a week away?</summary><p>Five tops, three or four bottoms, two pairs of shoes and one layer cover a week comfortably if everything goes together. With access to a washing machine, you can pack for four days and repeat.</p></details><details><summary>What is an outfit formula?</summary><p>A repeatable combination of garment types, such as a fitted top, wide trousers and a cropped jacket. Once you know two or three formulas that suit you, getting dressed becomes a matter of filling in the blanks.</p></details><details><summary>Can a tailor fix clothes that are too big?</summary><p>Usually. Shortening hems and sleeves and taking in the waist or side seams are simple jobs. Altering shoulders, or letting out a garment with no spare seam allowance, is difficult and often not worth it.</p></details></div></div></section>

<section class="tz-s tz-news"><div class="tz-w"><h2>The Threadz Fold</h2><p>One outfit formula and one fit tip by email, once a month.</p><a class="btn" href="mailto:hello@threadz.com?subject=The%20Threadz%20Fold&amp;body=Please%20subscribe%20me.">Subscribe by email</a></div></section>

